### B.2 Experiment 1: Finite-Sample Validity via Feature Ablation

Objective. Our first experiment establishes that the accounting identity derived in Equation ([7](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2601.20217#S3.E7 "In Proposition 1. ‣ 3 The Accounting Identity ‣ An Accounting Identity for Algorithmic Fairness")) holds approximately in finite samples typical of real-world applications. We use *feature ablation* to capture the effect of improving accuracy: we vary the number of features from just one to all, and train each model with access to those features.

Setup. We train four standard classification algorithms on each dataset: logistic regression, decision trees, random forests, and gradient boosting. For each model, we train a new instance for including each additional feature (where we treat each one-hot encoded variable as a new feature). For easy of deciphiring the figures, we include the models trained on the first few features and then multiples of 10 features. We incorporate features in the order they are listed in the dataset, except we exclude the sensitive feature. All models are recalibrated using isotonic regression to ensure global calibration holds.

Results. Panel (1) of each of Figures [1](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2601.20217#S4.F1 "Figure 1 ‣ 4 Empirical Analysis ‣ An Accounting Identity for Algorithmic Fairness")-[4](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2601.20217#A2.F4 "Figure 4 ‣ B.3 Experiment 2: Fairness Methods Reallocate Unfairness ‣ Appendix B Detailed Overview of Experimental Results ‣ An Accounting Identity for Algorithmic Fairness") plots, for each respective dataset, model, and number of features, the estimated total unfairness δ^B+δ^C\hat{\delta}\_{B}+\hat{\delta}\_{C} against model mean squared error. The dashed black line shows the theoretical prediction from Equation ([7](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2601.20217#S3.E7 "In Proposition 1. ‣ 3 The Accounting Identity ‣ An Accounting Identity for Algorithmic Fairness")). Across all four datasets and all model types, the empirical values closely track the theoretical prediction. In particular, as MSE decrease, total unfairness moves towareds 0 across each plot. Panel (2) in each of Figure [1](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2601.20217#S4.F1 "Figure 1 ‣ 4 Empirical Analysis ‣ An Accounting Identity for Algorithmic Fairness")-[4](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2601.20217#A2.F4 "Figure 4 ‣ B.3 Experiment 2: Fairness Methods Reallocate Unfairness ‣ Appendix B Detailed Overview of Experimental Results ‣ An Accounting Identity for Algorithmic Fairness") shows how the composition of unfairness changes as we increase the number of features. As the models are allowed to use increasing numbers of features, the total unfairness decreases roughly monotonically, but the relative shares of δC\delta\_{C} and δB\delta\_{B} shift, as well as sometimes their signs, shift.

